Jimmy_B wrote:

> My spacing convention is shaped by a set of related observations:
> - Blank lines separating logically separate blocks are good
> - Blank lines all over the place make blank lines separating blocks
>   worthless
> - A line containing a single punctuator at the same indentation level
>   as before it has the same effect as a blank line

I go even further than what you mentino here: I don't use blank lines at
all, except to separate "real" program blocks, like functions or
procedures.

In my opinion, where there is a blank line, there is a comment missing.
Every single blank line can and should be filled with a comment. If a
comment isn't useful, neither is a blank line, since no new part of the
code starts there.

Example:

* The following function translates into capitals
function capital ( parameters )
...
endfunction

* The following function prints sevaral pages
function print_pages
* Print page one
...
* Print page two
...
endfunction

Some people would intersperse empty lines before the "Print page x"
lines, or before "endfunction", or even after the "function" lines. I
find these unnecessary. Not using too many blank lines also has the
advantage that almost every function will fit on one screen of program
text, which greatly improves your grasp of what a function actually
does.

The example you give lateron is very good.

When I program, I go as far as to introduce macros to shorten commands
so I can fit things like the example you give on one line.



